WEBVTT

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:09.220
Glad you're here. Welcome to Models &amp; Agents, episode 83 for June 17th, 2026. Lots of news to walk through.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:21.480
Let's start with what actually matters. OpenAI's new deployment simulation technique replays real user requests against unreleased models to surface undesired behaviors before launch.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:32.060
OpenAI released research showing how deployment simulation estimates real-world failure rates by running candidate models on de-identified past conversations.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:41.240
The method replays recent user requests through candidate models and grades completions under realistic conditions rather than static benchmarks alone.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:52.060
It extends to agentic coding by simulating tool calls and multi-turn interactions, while traditional red teaming remains essential for rare high severity risks.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:00.220
The approach reportedly achieves a 1.5 times median multiplicative error on held out deployment data.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.260
Builders working on frontier systems should watch how labs fold these simulation results into future release decisions.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:15.120
This work continues the ongoing conversation around evaluation standardization that we have followed in recent episodes.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:25.520
Anthropic published fresh data showing cloud code task value rose 27% in six months while success rates stayed consistent across domains.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:35.020
The data track performance on code related tasks over the half year period. Success metrics held steady even as task value increased.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.460
The findings cover multiple domains without reported drops in reliability.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:45.700
Observers can compare these trends against other labs releasing similar longitudinal agent metrics.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:57.060
Microsoft research introduced next latent prediction which adds a self supervised objective so transformers predict their own next latent state alongside the next token.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:05.720
The method creates compact belief states that improve representation learning and enables recursive multi-step look ahead during inference.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:14.100
On top of standard next token training it delivers up to 3.3 times faster inference through self speculative decoding.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:21.180
The pre print includes code and a blog post for teams training long context or reasoning models.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:27.980
This development sits within the current focus on inference economics and test time compute improvements.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:40.280
Many max released MSA a two branch block sparse attention mechanism built on grouped query attention that selects top key value blocks via a lightweight index branch.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:51.060
The technique matches standard accuracy on downstream benchmarks while cutting per token attention compute by 28.4 times at 1 million token context length.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:59.860
It was trained inside a 109 billion parameter Mster of experts model using a 3 trillion token budget.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:07.780
Inference teams handling long context can evaluate whether the block selection overhead is offset by the compute savings.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:18.600
Researchers proposed rep select for robust LLM unlearning by collapsing top principal components of weight gradients before each unlearning update.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:25.240
The method isolates forget set specific representations through this principal component collapse step.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:30.500
Test spanned biohazardous knowledge alongside abusive tendency categories.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:37.220
It delivered 4 to 50 times larger reduction in post relearning accuracy versus strong baselines.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:44.440
Experiments ran on Lama 3, Quinn 3.5, Gemma 4 and Deepseek V2 light.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.600
The approach stayed robust against few shot prompting attacks.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:54.700
Safety teams can examine the representation selectivity approach for deeper unlearning guarantees.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:08.180
Mem slides introduces a hierarchical memory architecture for personalized slide generation agents that separates long term user profile memory, session level working memory, and reusable tool memory.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:15.860
Scoped slide local revision lets the agent edit only affected regions instead of regenerating entire decks.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:24.500
Experiments showed user profile memory improves persona alignment and tool memory improves closed loop modification reliability.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:32.280
Presentation automation teams can explore the memory design to cut regeneration costs in multi turn workflows.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:40.360
This agent memory work builds directly on the reliability questions for long horizon agents we discussed yesterday.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:52.220
OPD Evolver presents a slow fast co-evolution framework that trains agents to read, use, write, and maintain experience across a four level memory hierarchy.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:05:05.900
On policy self distillation transfers these abilities into the deployable policy and the system outperformed prior memory augmented agents by up to 11.5% on multi-domain benchmarks.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:15.020
Developers building self improving agents should examine how outcome calibrated attribution prevents low value memory accumulation.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:31.040
A study of single step rooting across a 110 agent and 584 tool enterprise catalog showed writing F1 dropping 16 to 23 points as catalog size scaled from 10 to 110 agents.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:38.100
Embedding based shortlisting recovered 10 to 17 points on real traffic despite lower absolute performance.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:45.540
Teams operating large tool catalogs should test shortlisting layers before routing accuracy degrades further.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:58.920
AI patient arena provides an EHR grounded multi turn evaluation framework spanning eight clinical competence directions across 437 primary patients plus out of distribution cohorts.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:09.760
Current model score well on questioning skills and ethical conduct yet show persistent weaknesses in handling ambiguity information coverage and diagnostic reasoning.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:21.480
The framework surfaces interaction failures such as repetitive questioning that static benchmarks miss clinical AI team should adopt similar workflow oriented testing before deployment.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:33.080
Prompt MN defines a lightweight pseudo prompting DSL that annotates natural language with compact percent prefix directives for roles goals constraints and outputs.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:40.500
Semantic resolution allows authors to write directives in any order while models interpret them by function.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:50.400
Early tests across Claude Gemini and GPT class models showed reliable parsing of complex structures without fine tuning.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.900
Prompt engineers working on agentic workflows can adopt the syntax to reduce context ambiguity.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:10.640
Researchers address the train inference mismatch and token to token editing for diffusion language models by generating training corruptions from the model's own draft outputs instead of random vocabulary noise.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:21.380
A short Laura continued pre-training pass improved accuracy while lowering unnecessary edit intensity on L-L-A-D-A 2.1 mini.

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:28.260
The change mitigates specific failure modes such as final digit transcription errors after correct reasoning.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:35.040
Defusion model practitioners should test self generated corruption schedules when tuning token editors.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:43.420
Okay, let's pop the hood on speculative decoding because everyone talks about it as simply running a small draft model then verifying.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:52.080
In practice, the technique is a balanced pipeline whose gains depend on acceptance rate statistics and KB cache reuse patterns.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:08:01.880
The draft model proposes several tokens in one forward pass while the target model verifies them in parallel and accepts the longest correct prefix.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:12.620
Higher acceptance rates come from training the draft on the target's output distribution, yet each extra draft token still costs compute that must be amortized.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:23.540
Real speed-ups of two to three times appear most reliably on repetitive or structured text where acceptance lengths stay above 2.5 tokens on average.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:31.580
On highly creative generation, the acceptance rate often drops below 1.8 and the overhead can erase gains.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:41.540
Measure acceptance length on your target workload first because if it stays under 2.0 tokens, you are better off with standard decoding or continuous batching.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:50.740
Test open AI's deployment simulation approach on your own evaluation set by replaying recent prompts against a held out model checkpoint.

00:08:51.740 --> 00:09:00.380
Experiment with next latent prediction by adding the latent state prediction loss to a small transformer training run and measuring inference speed up.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:09.440
Prototype a MEM slide style memory hierarchy in your slide generation agent to see whether scoped local revision reduces token spend.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:18.200
Try prompt MN syntax on a complex agent prompt to check whether the type directives reduce downstream misinterpretation.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:26.620
More labs are expected to publish deployment simulation or synthetic traffic evaluation results following open AI's release.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:35.240
Continued work on sparse attention and latent prediction objectives will likely appear in upcoming long context model training runs.

